  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.3', 'Particle Model of Matter', 'What is the specific latent heat of vaporisation?', 'The energy needed to change 1 kg from liquid to gas.', 17),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.3', 'Particle Model of Matter', 'Why does temperature stay constant during a change of state?', 'The energy is used to break bonds between particles, not to increase kinetic energy.', 18),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.3', 'Particle Model of Matter', 'What is melting?', 'Changing from solid to liquid.', 19),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.3', 'Particle Model of Matter', 'What is evaporation?', 'Changing from liquid to gas at the surface of a liquid, below its boiling point.', 20),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.3', 'Particle Model of Matter', 'What is condensation?', 'Changing from gas to liquid.', 21),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.3', 'Particle Model of Matter', 'What is sublimation?', 'Changing directly from solid to gas.', 22),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.3', 'Particle Model of Matter', 'Are changes of state physical or chemical?', 'Physical, because they are reversible and the substance keeps its chemical properties.', 23),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.3', 'Particle Model of Matter', 'Is mass conserved when a substance changes state?', 'Yes.', 24),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.3', 'Particle Model of Matter', 'How do gas particles exert pressure?', 'They collide with the walls of the container, exerting a force.', 25),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.3', 'Particle Model of Matter', 'What happens to gas pressure when temperature increases (constant volume)?', 'Pressure increases because particles move faster and collide more often and with more force.', 26),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.3', 'Particle Model of Matter', 'What is absolute zero?', '−273 °C (0 K), the lowest possible temperature, where particles have the minimum kinetic energy.', 27),
