  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.3', 'Particle Model of Matter', 'What happens to gas pressure when volume decreases (constant temperature)?', 'Pressure increases because particles hit the walls more often.', 29),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.3', 'Particle Model of Matter', 'What is the equation linking pressure and volume at constant temperature?', 'p₁V₁ = p₂V₂.', 30),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'What is the structure of an atom?', 'A small, dense, positively charged nucleus containing protons and neutrons, surrounded by electrons.', 1),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'What are the relative charges of protons, neutrons and electrons?', 'Proton +1, neutron 0, electron −1.', 2),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'What are the relative masses of protons, neutrons and electrons?', 'Proton 1, neutron 1, electron about 1/1840.', 3),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'What is the atomic number?', 'The number of protons in the nucleus.', 4),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'What is the mass number?', 'The total number of protons and neutrons in the nucleus.', 5),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'What is an isotope?', 'Atoms of the same element with the same number of protons but different numbers of neutrons.', 6),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'What is radioactive decay?', 'The random process in which an unstable nucleus emits radiation to become more stable.', 7),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'What is an alpha particle?', 'Two protons and two neutrons (a helium nucleus).', 8),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'What is a beta-minus particle?', 'A fast-moving electron emitted from the nucleus when a neutron turns into a proton.', 9),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'What is a positron?', 'A particle with the same mass as an electron but a positive charge, emitted in beta-plus decay.', 10),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'What is gamma radiation?', 'High-frequency electromagnetic radiation emitted from the nucleus.', 11),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'What is the penetrating power of alpha, beta and gamma?', 'Alpha is stopped by paper, beta by a few mm of aluminium, gamma is reduced by thick lead or concrete.', 12),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'What is the ionising power of alpha, beta and gamma?', 'Alpha is highly ionising, beta moderately, gamma weakly.', 13),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'What happens in alpha decay?', 'The mass number decreases by 4 and the atomic number decreases by 2.', 14),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'What happens in beta-minus decay?', 'The mass number stays the same and the atomic number increases by 1.', 15),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'What happens in gamma emission?', 'No change to mass number or atomic number; the nucleus loses energy.', 16),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'What is half-life?', 'The time taken for the number of radioactive nuclei (or activity) to halve.', 17),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'What is activity?', 'The rate at which a source decays, measured in becquerels (Bq).', 18),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'A sample has activity 800 Bq and half-life 2 hours. What is the activity after 6 hours?', 'Three half-lives: 800 → 400 → 200 → 100 Bq.', 19),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'What is background radiation?', 'Radiation that is always around us from natural and man-made sources.', 20),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'Name three sources of background radiation.', 'Radon gas, rocks, cosmic rays, food, and medical X-rays.', 21),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'What is irradiation?', 'Exposing an object to radiation; the object does not become radioactive.', 22),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'What is contamination?', 'When radioactive atoms get onto or into an object.', 23),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'How can workers be protected from radiation?', 'Shielding, keeping distance, limiting exposure time, and monitoring doses.', 24),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'How is radiation used in medicine?', 'Radiotherapy to kill cancer cells, tracers to diagnose problems, and sterilising equipment.', 25),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'What did the plum pudding model suggest?', 'The atom was a ball of positive charge with electrons embedded in it.', 26),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'What did the alpha scattering experiment show?', 'Most of the atom is empty space and there is a small, dense, positive nucleus.', 27),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'How did Bohr improve the atomic model?', 'He suggested electrons orbit the nucleus in fixed energy levels (shells).', 28),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'What happens when an electron moves to a higher energy level?', 'It absorbs electromagnetic radiation.', 29),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'What is nuclear fission?', 'Splitting a large nucleus into smaller nuclei, releasing energy and neutrons.', 30),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.5', 'Forces', 'What is a resultant force?', 'The single force that has the same effect as all the forces acting on an object.', 1),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.5', 'Forces', 'How do you calculate the resultant of two forces in the same direction?', 'Add them.', 2),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.5', 'Forces', 'How do you calculate the resultant of two forces in opposite directions?', 'Subtract the smaller from the larger; the resultant acts in the direction of the larger.', 3),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.5', 'Forces', 'What happens if the resultant force is zero?', 'The object stays at rest or continues at constant velocity.', 4),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.5', 'Forces', 'What is a contact force?', 'A force that acts when objects are touching, e.g. friction, air resistance, tension and normal contact force.', 5),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.5', 'Forces', 'What is a non-contact force?', 'A force that acts at a distance, e.g. gravity, magnetism and electrostatic force.', 6),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.5', 'Forces', 'What is a free-body force diagram?', 'A diagram showing all the forces acting on a single object, with arrows showing size and direction.', 7),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.5', 'Forces', 'How can forces be resolved?', 'By splitting a force into two perpendicular components, e.g. horizontal and vertical.', 8),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.5', 'Forces', 'What is a moment?', 'The turning effect of a force: moment = force × perpendicular distance from the pivot.', 9),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.5', 'Forces', 'What is the unit of a moment?', 'Newton metre (N m).', 10),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.5', 'Forces', 'What is speed?', 'The distance travelled per unit time: speed = distance ÷ time.', 11),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.5', 'Forces', 'What is velocity?', 'Speed in a given direction.', 12),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.5', 'Forces', 'What is acceleration?', 'The rate of change of velocity: a = (v − u) ÷ t.', 13),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.5', 'Forces', 'What is the unit of acceleration?', 'm/s².', 14),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.5', 'Forces', 'What does the gradient of a distance–time graph show?', 'Speed.', 15),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.5', 'Forces', 'What does a horizontal line on a distance–time graph show?', 'The object is stationary.', 16),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.5', 'Forces', 'What does the gradient of a velocity–time graph show?', 'Acceleration.', 17),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.5', 'Forces', 'What does the area under a velocity–time graph show?', 'Distance travelled.', 18),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.5', 'Forces', 'What does a horizontal line on a velocity–time graph show?', 'Constant velocity.', 19),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.5', 'Forces', 'What is the equation linking v, u, a and x?', 'v² − u² = 2ax.', 20),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.5', 'Forces', 'A car accelerates from 10 m/s to 30 m/s in 5 s. What is its acceleration?', '(30 − 10) ÷ 5 = 4 m/s².', 21),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.5', 'Forces', 'What is the acceleration due to gravity near Earth''s surface?', 'About 10 m/s² (9.8 m/s²).', 22),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.5', 'Forces', 'Give typical speeds for walking, cycling and a car in town.', 'Walking about 1.5 m/s, cycling about 6 m/s, a car in town about 13 m/s.', 23),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.5', 'Forces', 'What is Newton''s first law?', 'An object stays at rest or moves at constant velocity unless a resultant force acts on it.', 24),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.5', 'Forces', 'What is elastic deformation?', 'When an object returns to its original shape after the force is removed.', 25),
